tisfied\",\"summary\":\"nginx was installed, configured to terminate TLS on 8443, enabled/started, and a curl -vk request completed a full TLSv1.3 handshake returning HTTP 200. The after-executor listening-sockets snapshot independently confirms nginx bound to 0.0.0.0:8443 and [::]:8443.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9254d83e6a734bd3\",\"cmd-8aed686faa0f4473\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Certificate is renewed automatically before it expires\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A renewal script and a systemd timer (cert-renew.timer, 30s interval) were installed and shown active. However, both certificate reissuances captured in the timeline (03:09:59 and 03:11:04) came from manual invocations of renew-cert.sh by the executor, not from the timer firing — the timer was only enabled afterward (03:11:17), and no automatic (timer-triggered) renewal was observed in the evidence before the turn ended. This is supporting configuration only, not an observed automatic renewal event.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-582f6a73ad644d4f\",\"cmd-285fe4c862fd4422\",\"cmd-9c2f6c7cb9ae42f8\",\"cmd-0adb698d92744884\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Service picks up each new certificate while continuing to answer requests (no dropped availability across a renewal)\",\"status\":\"indeterminate\",\"summary\":\"A background watch loop polling every 5s showed continuous HTTP 200 responses, but only ever observed a single certificate serial (6A9B8848) throughout the captured window — no automatic renewal/reload event occurred during the observation period, so the transition (old cert -> reload -> new cert, with availability preserved) was never directly captured. The executor's own report explicitly states this evidence was not obtained before the turn ended.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-db57d0e733bf4e99\",\"cmd-76916fb159ec402e\",\"cmd-0adb698d92744884\"]}],\"confidence\":0.45,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Mutations were generally scoped and purposeful: nginx install, local CA creation, TLS conf, renew-cert.sh (iterated twice, second version correcting ownership/permissions — a legitimate corrective action, cmd-9c2f6c7cb9ae42f8), and the systemd timer/service pair were all directly tied to the task. No failed or conflicting services appear in the after-executor snapshot, and no security controls were weakened (SELinux stayed enforcing, cert ownership tightened to nginx:nginx). The main hygiene gap is unreverted evidence-collection residue: /tmp/watch.sh, watch.log, watch.out, watch.pid, plus a background watch process (cmd-db57d0e733bf4e99) left running, and /tmp/ca.log and /tmp/nginx_install.log installation logs never cleaned up, all visible in the after-executor temporary-files diff. These are low-risk, non-destructive leftovers rather than abandoned or conflicting state, but they were not cleaned up before the turn ended.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-db57d0e733bf4e99\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"cmd-9c2f6c7cb9ae42f8\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"HTTPS is confirmed live on node1:8443 with a locally-issued certificate served over TLSv1.3 (satisfied). Automatic renewal infrastructure (script + systemd timer) was installed and shown active, but every certificate reissuance actually observed in the timeline was a manual script invocation by the executor, not a timer-triggered event — no automatic renewal was captured before the turn ended, so this is only partially satisfied as configuration. The most safety-critical requirement — the service continuing to answer requests through a live certificate swap — was never observed at all; the watch loop ran but never caught a renewal in progress, leaving this requirement indeterminate. The executor's own final report is consistent with this: it self-reports status 'blocked' and explicitly flags that live renewal-under-load evidence and the mandatory per-node restart protocol were not completed.\",\"limitations\":[\"No automatic (timer-triggered) certificate renewal was captured in the evidence window; both observed renewals were manual script runs.\",\"No observation exists of the service serving requests continuously through an actual certificate swap/reload.\",\"The provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol was not run for node1: no reboot was performed, and boot-identity is identical between before-prepare and after-executor snapshots, so no restart/recovery behavior can be assessed.\",\"The task was explicitly force-ended mid-turn per the executor's own report, so the observed evidence set is incomplete by the executor's own account.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Serve HTTPS on node1:8443 with a valid certificate","status":"satisfied","summary":"nginx was installed, configured to terminate TLS on 8443, enabled/started, and a curl -vk request completed a full TLSv1.3 handshake returning HTTP 200. The after-executor listening-sockets snapshot independently confirms nginx bound to 0.0.0.0:8443 and [::]:8443.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9254d83e6a734bd3","cmd-8aed686faa0f4473","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Certificate is renewed automatically before it expires","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"A renewal script and a systemd timer (cert-renew.timer, 30s interval) were installed and shown active. However, both certificate reissuances captured in the timeline (03:09:59 and 03:11:04) came from manual invocations of renew-cert.sh by the executor, not from the timer firing — the timer was only enabled afterward (03:11:17), and no automatic (timer-triggered) renewal was observed in the evidence before the turn ended. This is supporting configuration only, not an observed automatic renewal event.","evidence_ids":["cmd-582f6a73ad644d4f","cmd-285fe4c862fd4422","cmd-9c2f6c7cb9ae42f8","cmd-0adb698d92744884"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement"